ition="float"><label>Fig.&#x02002;1</label><caption><p>Histological findings of esophageal squamous papilloma. <bold>a)</bold> Representative histological features of ESP. The ESP shows a papillary projection lined with acanthotic squamous epithelium (original magnification: &#x000d7;10). <bold>b)</bold> Marked neutrophil infiltration in the whole tumor epithelium (original magnification: &#x000d7;25). <bold>c)</bold> Koilocytosis in ESP is vague compared with dysplasias of uterine cervix (original magnification: &#x000d7;25).</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="AHC05052f01"/></fig><fig id="F2" position="float"><label>Fig.&#x02002;2</label><caption><p>Correlation of tumor location with neutrophil infiltration in the tumor epithelium (p&#x0003c;0.003).</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="AHC05052f02"/></fig><fig id="F3" position="float"><label>Fig.&#x02002;3</label><caption><p>Representative findings of MIB-1 immunohistochemical stains of esophageal squamous papillomas (original magnification: &#x000d7;10). <bold>a)</bold> A papilloma case with low MIB-1 index. <bold>b)</bold> A papilloma with high MIB-1 index.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="AHC05052f03"/></fig><fig id="F4" position="float"><label>Fig.&#x02002;4</label><caption><p>Results of PCR analysis to detect human papilloma virus (HPV) infection in ESPs. <bold>a)</bold> PCR products using consensus primers-PCR. Lanes 1-4: PCR products from four cases are HPV-positive. Lane 5, negative control (no DNA); Lanes 6, positive control (synthetic DNA). <bold>b)</bold> PCR products digested by restriction enzyme Afa I. Lanes 1&#x02013;4 indicate HPV subtype 6.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="AHC05052f04"/></fig><table-wrap id="T1" position="float"><label>Table&#x02002;1</label><caption><p>Distribution of esophageal squamous papillomas</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><thead><tr><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Location of ESPs in the esophagus</th><th align="center" valign="middle" rowspan="1